 567: def inherited_verdict(src, base_src):
 568:     """True when this file's verdict badge is BYTE-IDENTICAL to its base's.
 569: 
 570:     â­ This is the sound test for the HFrEF green-badge failure, and choosing it
 571:     took a measurement. The obvious check - 'require the payload to name its own
 572:     app' - is NOT sound: 713 of the 759 corpus apps ship a green STABLE verdict
 573:     and NONE of them carries a self-reference, so that rule would block almost
 574:     the whole corpus while proving nothing about inheritance.
 575: 
 576:     Byte-identity against the base does prove it. Verified 2026-07-23:
 577:     HFREF_NMA_FULL_REVIEW.html's payload is byte-identical to
 578:     GLP1_CVOT_REVIEW.html's - and GLP1_CVOT is exactly the base the 2026-07-23
 579:     audit identified HFrEF as a clone of. The green badge was inherited, not
 580:     earned. A genuinely recomputed verdict differs in at least one count.
 581: 
 582:     Corpus context (advisory, reported by --all): 700 of 750 apps carrying a
 583:     verdict share their payload with at least one other app; one single payload
 584:     is shared by 436 apps. That is a corpus-wide debt, not something a per-clone
 585:     gate can fix - but it must never be silently normalised."""
 586:     p = verdict_payload(src)
 587:     return bool(p) and p == verdict_payload(base_src) and is_green_verdict(p)
